.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt QCOM_GENI_I2C 4 aarch64
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm qcom_geni_i2c
.Nd "Qualcomm GENI I2C controller driver"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following lines in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device iicbus"
.Cd "device qcom_geni_i2c"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
qcom_geni_i2c_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides an
.Xr iicbus 4
for each serial engine of a Qualcomm Snapdragon system-on-chip's
Generic Interface (GENI, or QUP) that the firmware has set up for I2C:
with the I2C protocol firmware loaded, its clocks on and the bus speed
configured, which the driver keeps.
Transfers go through the engine's FIFOs and are polled.
.Pp
ACPI does not describe the chips on the buses.
On known boards, recognized by their SMBIOS system maker and product and
the bus's ACPI
.Va _UID ,
the driver declares them as
.Xr device.hints 5
for the
.Xr iicbus 4 ,
unless hints already name the driver.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver attaches to ACPI devices with hardware ID
.Qq QCOM0610
whose engine runs the I2C protocol.
It has been tested on the Radxa Dragon Q8B
(Qualcomm Snapdragon 8cx Gen 3, SC8280XP), where it declares the board's
ST M41T11 real-time clock for
.Xr ds13rtc 4 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr iic 4 ,
.Xr iicbus 4 ,
.Xr qcom_scm 4 ,
.Xr i2c 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
driver and this manual page were written by
.An James Kane .
.Sh BUGS
Only the bus speed the firmware configured is supported, and the
controller's DMA modes are not used.
